const __vite__mapDeps=(i,m=__vite__mapDeps,d=(m.f||(m.f=["assets/Panchakosha3DViewer-u9Kxg0yU.js","assets/rolldown-runtime-XKyaHtl9.js","assets/vendor-framer-HPtay4w-.js","assets/vendor-three-DMppALgn.js","assets/KoshaLotus-BPzPpVQ_.js","assets/useInView-6t3WHOAv.js"])))=>i.map(i=>d[i]);
import{r as e}from"./rolldown-runtime-XKyaHtl9.js";import{i as t,r as n}from"./vendor-framer-HPtay4w-.js";import{t as r,u as i}from"./index-Dy_4qWHW.js";import{t as a}from"./LazyViewport-CT0c0XP7.js";import{t as o}from"./verses-Egs9pT8l.js";import{t as s}from"./FullWidthVerseBand-k2qLPXXo.js";import{t as c}from"./UpanishadPageShell-D1UipKqM.js";var l=e(t(),1),u={id:3,devanagariNumeral:`३`,title:`Fearlessness`,subtitle:`Abhaya — The Root of Resilience`,sanskritTitle:`अभयम्`,epigraph:`यदा ह्येवैष एतस्मिन्नदृश्ये... अभयं प्रतिष्ठां विन्दते — When one finds a resting place in the Invisible... one attains fearlessness. (Taittirīya Upaniṣad 2.7.1, author's rendering)`,content:[{type:`prose`,text:`Fear, in the Upanishadic analysis, is not simply an emotion to be managed. It is a diagnostic — a signal of a specific metaphysical error. The Bṛhadāraṇyaka Upaniṣad contains one of the most striking analyses of fear in world literature. In 1.4.2, the text describes the primordial being (Ātman, here in a cosmogonic narrative) who, having created the appearance of a world, 'was afraid, for one who is alone is afraid.' Then the text adds a crucial observation: 'He reflected: since there is nothing other than myself, of what am I afraid? Thereupon his fear departed. For of what should he have been afraid? It is from a second that fear arises' — dvitīyād vai bhayaṃ bhavati.`},{type:`verse_ref`,verseId:`brhadaranyaka-1-4-2`},{type:`prose`,text:`Śaṅkara, in his Bhāṣya on this passage, reads the verse as establishing a direct causal link between the perception of duality (dvitīya-darśana) and the arising of fear. Fear, in this analysis, is not irrational. It is perfectly rational — given the premise that there is an 'other' that could threaten, harm, or destroy. The error lies not in the fear response itself but in the ontological premise that generates it: the belief that one is a separate, bounded, vulnerable entity facing a world of separate, potentially hostile entities. When this premise is recognised as false — when one sees that there is no 'second' (advitīya) — fear has no ground to arise.`},{type:`prose`,text:`The Taittirīya Upaniṣad develops this theme across two key passages. At 2.4.1, the text declares that Brahman (the ultimate reality) is characterised by satyam (truth), jñānam (knowledge), and anantam (infinitude). Later, at 2.9.1, the text describes the one who knows Brahman as attaining 'the supreme' and being freed from fear: 'He is not tormented by the thought: why did I not do good? Why did I do evil? One who knows thus liberates the self from both' (author's rendering). The passage situates fearlessness not as emotional suppression but as the fruit of knowledge — specifically, the knowledge that one's deepest identity is not the empirical agent who can succeed or fail, do good or evil, but the Ātman that underlies all such empirical distinctions.`},{type:`verse_ref`,verseId:`taittiriya-2-9-1`},{type:`prose`,text:`Radhakrishnan (1953) reads these passages as describing abhaya — fearlessness — as the natural state of the knower of Brahman, not an achievement but a recognition. This distinction is important. In most modern psychological frameworks, fear reduction is understood as a process: one learns to tolerate, regulate, or extinguish fear responses through exposure, cognitive restructuring, or neurobiological intervention. The Upanishadic framework does not deny the reality of fear at the empirical level but relocates the discussion to a different register: fear is ultimately a consequence of misidentification (adhyāsa, in Śaṅkara's terminology), and its definitive resolution lies not in emotional technique but in knowledge of one's true nature.`},{type:`prose`,text:`A cautious connection to modern research may be noted. Fear-extinction research in neuroscience (see Milad & Quirk, 2012, for a review) demonstrates that fearful associations can be weakened through new learning that does not erase the original memory but creates a competing, inhibitory association. The Upanishadic model of abhaya could be read as proposing something structurally analogous at the existential level: the knowledge of Ātman does not erase the empirical self's vulnerability but creates a 'competing' understanding — an alternative identity-ground — that is inherently beyond threat. This parallel should be held lightly. The Upanishadic teaching operates at the level of ontological identity, not conditioned fear responses. But the structural similarity — new knowledge competing with old fear-based frameworks — is suggestive.`},{type:`resilience_insight`,title:`Fear as Diagnostic, Not Just Symptom`,text:`The Bṛhadāraṇyaka's analysis reframes fear as a signal of perceived separateness rather than a problem to be eliminated. In resilience terms, this suggests that chronic fearfulness may reflect not merely 'threat sensitivity' but a deeper relationship to one's own identity — a lived experience of being fundamentally separate and therefore fundamentally vulnerable. Resilience, in this reading, involves not just managing fear but investigating the sense of self that generates it.`},{type:`modern_parallel`,title:`Terror Management Theory`,text:`Sheldon Solomon, Jeff Greenberg, and Tom Pyszczynski's Terror Management Theory (1986, building on Ernest Becker's The Denial of Death, 1973) proposes that awareness of mortality generates a profound existential anxiety that humans manage through cultural worldviews and self-esteem. The Upanishadic analysis in Bṛhadāraṇyaka 1.4.2 can be read as a parallel diagnosis — fear arises from perceived vulnerability — but with a radically different prescription. TMT proposes symbolic defences against death anxiety; the Upanishads propose that the deepest self does not die at all. Whether this Upanishadic claim is 'true' is beyond empirical adjudication, but it represents a fundamentally different strategy for addressing existential fear.`},{type:`reflect`,prompt:`The Bṛhadāraṇyaka suggests that fear arises from the perception of a 'second' — something separate from oneself that could be threatening. In your own life, are your deepest fears rooted in a sense of separateness — from others, from security, from belonging? What would shift if that separateness were not the final word?`}],sources:[`Bṛhadāraṇyaka Upaniṣad 1.4.2`,`Taittirīya Upaniṣad 2.4.1, 2.7.1, 2.9.1`,`Śaṅkara. Bṛhadāraṇyaka Upaniṣad Bhāṣya on 1.4.2.`,`Radhakrishnan, S. The Principal Upanishads. George Allen & Unwin, 1953.`,`Milad, M.R. & Quirk, G.J. 'Fear Extinction as a Model for Translational Neuroscience.' Annual Review of Psychology 63 (2012): 129–151.`,`Solomon, S., Greenberg, J., & Pyszczynski, T. 'A Terror Management Theory of Social Behavior.' Advances in Experimental Social Psychology 24 (1991): 93–159.`,`Becker, Ernest. The Denial of Death. Free Press, 1973.`],wordCount:820},d={id:9,devanagariNumeral:`९`,title:`Five Sheaths`,subtitle:`The Pañcakośa Model and Resilience Across Dimensions (Taittirīya Upaniṣad)`,sanskritTitle:`पञ्चकोशविवेकः`,epigraph:`तस्माद्वा एतस्मादात्मन आकाशः सम्भूतः — From this Self, space arose. (Taittirīya 2.1.1, author's rendering)`,content:[{type:`prose`,text:`The Taittirīya Upaniṣad, in its Brahmānanda Vallī (second chapter), offers one of the most systematic models of the human person in all of Indian philosophy: the Pañcakośa — the Five Sheaths. The model describes the self as layered, like concentric shells or sheaths (kośa), each progressively subtler, each enveloping and pervading the one before it. At the centre — or rather, as the ground of all five — lies the Ātman, which is not itself a sheath but that which the sheaths conceal and express.`},{type:`prose`,text:`The five sheaths are: (1) Annamaya kośa — the sheath made of food (anna), i.e., the physical body. This is the most tangible layer of selfhood: bone, muscle, organ, tissue, nourished by food and returning to food at death. 'From food beings are born; by food, once born, they grow' (2.2.1, author's rendering). (2) Prāṇamaya kośa — the sheath made of vital breath (prāṇa). This is the energetic body: the five prāṇas (prāṇa, apāna, vyāna, udāna, samāna) that govern respiration, digestion, circulation, and all physiological processes. (3) Manomaya kośa — the sheath made of mind (manas). This is the mental body: the faculty of thought, emotion, imagination, and volition. (4) Vijñānamaya kośa — the sheath made of discernment (vijñāna). This is the intellective body: the faculty of discrimination, judgment, and self-reflective awareness. Śaṅkara identifies this kośa with buddhi — the charioteer of the Kaṭha model. (5) Ānandamaya kośa — the sheath made of bliss (ānanda). This is the subtlest experiential layer: the deep contentment that is glimpsed in dreamless sleep, in moments of self-forgetful absorption, and — according to the tradition — in the direct intuition of Brahman.`},{type:`verse_ref`,verseId:`taittiriya-2-1-1`},{type:`prose`,text:`Śaṅkara, in his Bhāṣya on the Taittirīya, is careful to point out that the Ātman is not identical with any of the five sheaths, including the ānandamaya kośa. Even bliss, as experienced, is a sheath — a covering that can obscure the pure being-awareness that lies 'within.' Rāmānuja, by contrast, reads the ānandamaya kośa as Brahman itself, not a mere sheath. This interpretive difference has significant implications for practice: in Śaṅkara's reading, even blissful experience must be transcended; in Rāmānuja's, bliss is the nature of the ultimate reality itself.`},{type:`prose`,text:`The Pañcakośa model can be mapped, with appropriate caution, onto a multi-dimensional model of resilience. Resilience is not a single faculty; it operates across domains. At the physical level (annamaya), resilience involves bodily health, adequate nutrition, sleep, and physical safety — the material foundations without which psychological resilience is severely compromised. At the energetic level (prāṇamaya), resilience involves the regulation of vitality: breathing patterns, energy management, the capacity to mobilise and recover. At the mental level (manomaya), resilience involves emotional regulation, cognitive flexibility, and the capacity to process adversity without being overwhelmed. At the intellective level (vijñānamaya), resilience involves meaning-making, self-reflective awareness, and the capacity to distinguish between productive and unproductive responses to difficulty. At the bliss level (ānandamaya), resilience involves access to deep contentment, purpose, and a sense of wholeness that transcends any particular circumstance.`},{type:`prose`,text:`The Taittirīya's third chapter — the Bhṛgu Vallī — enacts this progressive discovery dramatically. Bhṛgu, son of Varuṇa, asks his father: 'Teach me Brahman.' Varuṇa replies: 'That from which beings are born, by which they live, and into which they return — seek to know That. That is Brahman.' Varuṇa then adds: 'tapasā brahma vijijñāsasva — through tapas (focused effort, austerity, disciplined inquiry), seek to know Brahman' (3.1, author's rendering). Bhṛgu undertakes tapas and identifies Brahman successively with food, with prāṇa, with mind, with vijñāna, and finally with ānanda. Each identification is partial; each is surpassed. The structure mirrors the kośa model exactly: through disciplined inquiry, one moves inward from the grossest to the subtlest layer of experience.`},{type:`verse_ref`,verseId:`taittiriya-3-1-1`},{type:`prose`,text:`For resilience practice, each sheath suggests a domain of care. Practices for annamaya kośa: attend to nutrition, movement, rest — the foundational physical conditions of psychological health. Practices for prāṇamaya kośa: conscious breathing (prāṇāyāma), energy management, awareness of the body's vital rhythms. Practices for manomaya kośa: journaling, emotional processing, cognitive reappraisal, and the cultivation of śama (inner calm). Practices for vijñānamaya kośa: viveka (discrimination) practice — regularly asking 'Is this response aligned with my deepest values, or is it reactive?', contemplative study (svādhyāya), and self-inquiry (ātma-vicāra). Practices for ānandamaya kośa: silence, meditation, immersion in beauty, gratitude — practices that reconnect one with the ground of contentment that precedes and outlasts circumstantial happiness.`},{type:`resilience_insight`,title:`Multi-Layered Resilience`,text:`The Pañcakośa model implies that resilience collapse at one level (e.g., physical illness at the annamaya level) does not necessarily entail collapse at all levels. A person may be physically impaired yet mentally lucid, or emotionally turbulent yet grounded in a deep sense of meaning. Conversely, a breakdown in resilience at a subtler level — such as a loss of meaning (vijñānamaya) — can undermine functioning at all grosser levels. This multi-layered analysis resonates with socio-ecological models of resilience (Ungar, 2012) that emphasise the interplay between individual, relational, and contextual factors across multiple domains.`},{type:`modern_parallel`,title:`Biopsychosocial Models and the Kośas`,text:`The biopsychosocial model (Engel, 1977), foundational to modern health psychology, proposes that health and illness are determined by the interaction of biological, psychological, and social factors. The Pañcakośa model can be read as an anticipation of this multi-level approach — though with two additions that the biopsychosocial model lacks: the energetic (prāṇamaya) and the bliss (ānandamaya) dimensions. Whether these additions represent genuine insights or metaphysical assumptions beyond empirical validation is a matter of perspective. What both models share is the insistence that no single level of analysis is sufficient: a complete account of human well-being — and resilience — must be multi-dimensional.`},{type:`reflect`,prompt:`Consider your own resilience across the five sheaths. Where are you strongest? Where are you most neglected? Is there a sheath — physical, energetic, mental, intellective, or experiential — that you tend to ignore? What would it mean to attend to all five as a unified practice of self-care?`}],sources:[`Taittirīya Upaniṣad 2.1–2.5 (Pañcakośa), 3.1–3.6 (Bhṛgu Vallī)`,`Śaṅkara. Taittirīya Upaniṣad Bhāṣya on 2.1–2.5.`,`Rāmānuja. Śrī Bhāṣya (interpretation of ānandamaya kośa).`,`Radhakrishnan, S. The Principal Upanishads. George Allen & Unwin, 1953.`,`Olivelle, Patrick. Upaniṣads. Oxford University Press, 1996.`,`Ungar, Michael. 'Social Ecologies and Their Contribution to Resilience.' The Social Ecology of Resilience. Springer, 2012.`,`Engel, George. 'The Need for a New Medical Model.' Science 196 (1977): 129–136.`],wordCount:980},f=n(),p=(0,l.lazy)(()=>r(()=>import(`./Panchakosha3DViewer-u9Kxg0yU.js`),__vite__mapDeps([0,1,2,3]))),m=(0,l.lazy)(()=>r(()=>import(`./KoshaLotus-BPzPpVQ_.js`),__vite__mapDeps([4,1,2,5]))),h=new Map;o.forEach(e=>h.set(e.id,e));var g=i.find(e=>e.slug===`taittiriya`),_=i.find(e=>e.slug===`mandukya`),v=i.find(e=>e.slug===`aitareya`),y=[{id:`fearless`,label:`1. Fearlessness in Bliss (v. 2.4.1)`},{id:`koshas`,label:`2. The Five Sheaths (Koshas)`},{id:`bhrigu`,label:`3. Bhṛgu's Inquiry (v. 3.1)`}];function b(){let[e,t]=(0,l.useState)(`fearless`),n=h.get(`taittiriya-2-4-1`),r=h.get(`taittiriya-2-9-1`),i=h.get(`taittiriya-2-1-5`),o=h.get(`taittiriya-3-1`);return(0,f.jsxs)(c,{meta:g,prev:{title:_.title,path:_.path,devanagari:_.devanagari},next:{title:v.title,path:v.path,devanagari:v.devanagari,teaser:v.teaser},steps:y,activeStep:e,onStepChange:t,resilienceInsight:{title:`The Onion of Identity: Why Stress Breaks the Periphery, Not the Center`,text:`When we suffer burn-out, we assume our whole life is ruined. The Taittirīya reveals the human being as five concentric sheaths (*pañcakośa*): physical food body (Annamaya), vital energy (Prāṇamaya), emotional reactivity (Manomaya), intellect (Vijñānamaya), and unconditioned joy (Ānandamaya). Fatigue belongs to the food body; anxiety belongs to the emotional mind. The core witness is untouched by either.`},modernParallel:{title:`Biopsychosocial Model & Polyvagal Layering`,text:`George Engel's biopsychosocial framework partitions human distress across biological, psychological, and sociological layers. The Taittirīya system provided an identical diagnostic model 2,600 years ago: treat physical depletion at the food sheath, nervous exhaustion at the prāṇic sheath, and rumination at the cognitive sheath—while remembering that your foundational Self is unconditional wholeness.`},reflectPrompt:`Which layer of your experience is currently crying out for attention: physical nourishment, breath, emotional rest, or intellectual clarity?`,sources:[...u.sources,...d.sources],commentaryDrawer:{title:`Śaṅkara on the Five Sheaths and the Bhṛgu Vallī Pedagogy`,content:(0,f.jsxs)(f.Fragment,{children:[(0,f.jsx)(`p`,{children:`In his commentary on the *Brahmavallī*, Śaṅkara notes that the sequence of koshas proceeds from the grossest (*sthūla*) to the subtlest (*sūkṣma*). Just as one peels layers of husks to reveal the grain, the seeker peels through bodily and emotional identifications until only the luminous Ātman remains.`}),(0,f.jsx)(`p`,{children:`In the *Bhṛgu Vallī*, young Bhṛgu repeatedly returns to his father Varuṇa asking for the definition of Brahman. Varuṇa does not lecture; he commands: "Do tapas (deep investigative reflection)." Bhṛgu discovers layer by layer that food passes, breath passes, mind passes, but that bliss from which all beings arise and into which they return is the ultimate reality.`})]})},children:[(0,f.jsxs)(`section`,{id:`fearless`,className:`w-full max-w-[1000px] mx-auto space-y-8 flex flex-col items-center`,children:[(0,f.jsxs)(`div`,{className:`w-full p-8 sm:p-10 rounded-[20px] bg-[var(--surface)] border border-[var(--border-color)] shadow-sm text-center space-y-4`,children:[(0,f.jsx)(`span`,{className:`text-xs sm:text-sm uppercase tracking-widest text-[var(--gold)] font-[var(--font-marginalia)] font-bold block`,children:`७.१ · आनन्दं ब्रह्मणो विद्वान्`}),(0,f.jsx)(`h2`,{className:`font-[var(--font-heading-alt)] font-semibold text-[var(--text-primary)] text-balance mx-auto`,style:{fontSize:`clamp(1.75rem, 3.5vw, 2.75rem)`,lineHeight:1.2},children:`The Bliss That Conquers Fear`}),(0,f.jsx)(`div`,{className:`w-full max-w-[68ch] mx-auto text-left`,children:(0,f.jsx)(`p`,{className:`text-[var(--text-secondary)] leading-relaxed`,style:{fontSize:`clamp(1.05rem, 1.25vw, 1.25rem)`,lineHeight:1.75},children:`"He who knows the bliss of Brahman, whence words along with the mind turn back unable to attain, fears nothing from any quarter." The Taittirīya reveals that fearlessness is not emotional numbness, but grounding in primordial bliss (*Ānanda*).`})})]}),n&&(0,f.jsx)(s,{verse:n}),r&&(0,f.jsx)(s,{verse:r})]}),(0,f.jsxs)(`section`,{id:`koshas`,className:`w-full max-w-[1000px] mx-auto space-y-8 flex flex-col items-center`,children:[(0,f.jsxs)(`div`,{className:`w-full p-8 sm:p-10 rounded-[20px] bg-[var(--surface)] border border-[var(--border-color)] shadow-sm text-center space-y-4`,children:[(0,f.jsx)(`span`,{className:`text-xs sm:text-sm uppercase tracking-widest text-[var(--gold)] font-[var(--font-marginalia)] font-bold block`,children:`७.२ · पञ्चकोश विवेकः`}),(0,f.jsx)(`h2`,{className:`font-[var(--font-heading-alt)] font-semibold text-[var(--text-primary)] text-balance mx-auto`,style:{fontSize:`clamp(1.75rem, 3.5vw, 2.75rem)`,lineHeight:1.2},children:`3D Concentric Peeler of the Five Sheaths`}),(0,f.jsx)(`p`,{className:`text-xs sm:text-sm text-[var(--text-muted)] max-w-[60ch] mx-auto`,children:`Drag and peel through the 5 concentric layers (physical, vital, mental, wisdom, bliss) to uncover the radiant witness core.`})]}),(0,f.jsx)(l.Suspense,{fallback:(0,f.jsx)(`div`,{className:`h-96 rounded-2xl bg-[var(--surface-sunken)] animate-pulse`}),children:(0,f.jsx)(a,{minHeight:`450px`,children:(0,f.jsx)(p,{})})}),(0,f.jsx)(l.Suspense,{fallback:(0,f.jsx)(`div`,{className:`h-72 rounded-xl bg-[var(--surface-sunken)] animate-pulse`}),children:(0,f.jsx)(a,{minHeight:`300px`,children:(0,f.jsx)(m,{})})}),i&&(0,f.jsx)(s,{verse:i})]}),(0,f.jsxs)(`section`,{id:`bhrigu`,className:`w-full max-w-[1000px] mx-auto space-y-8 flex flex-col items-center`,children:[(0,f.jsxs)(`div`,{className:`w-full p-8 sm:p-10 rounded-[20px] bg-[var(--surface)] border border-[var(--border-color)] shadow-sm text-center space-y-4`,children:[(0,f.jsx)(`span`,{className:`text-xs sm:text-sm uppercase tracking-widest text-[var(--saffron)] font-[var(--font-marginalia)] font-bold block`,children:`७.३ · भृगु वल्ली तपस्`}),(0,f.jsx)(`h2`,{className:`font-[var(--font-heading-alt)] font-semibold text-[var(--text-primary)] text-balance mx-auto`,style:{fontSize:`clamp(1.75rem, 3.5vw, 2.75rem)`,lineHeight:1.2},children:`Bhṛgu's Empirical Investigation`}),(0,f.jsx)(`div`,{className:`w-full max-w-[68ch] mx-auto text-left`,children:(0,f.jsx)(`p`,{className:`text-[var(--text-secondary)] leading-relaxed`,style:{fontSize:`clamp(1.05rem, 1.25vw, 1.25rem)`,lineHeight:1.75},children:d.content[0]?.text})})]}),o&&(0,f.jsx)(s,{verse:o})]})]})}export{b as default};